#!/usr/bin/env -S uv run --script
# /// script
# requires-python = ">=3.10"
# dependencies = ["bibtexparser==2.1.0", "pybtex==0.26.1", "pyyaml"]
# ///
"""tools/bib-merge — merge the series' bibliographies into the pool's one file.

Every series keeps its own ``lectures/_static/quant-econ.bib``. The pool has
one: ``lectures/_static/quant-econ.bib``, generated by this tool from the
series' copies in ``mirror/`` and the rulings in ``sync/bib.yml``, and never
edited by hand. It is regenerated after every sync, as the pool lectures are
refreshed, so a key a series adds reaches the pool the next morning.

How a key is merged
-------------------
Each series' copy of an entry is read and NORMALISED first, then the copies
are compared. Normalising applies the house style's mechanical rules
(QuantEcon manual, "Writing a bibliography entry") and nothing else:

* field names and the entry type in lower case;
* whitespace runs collapsed to one space (one field per line);
* ``month`` and the fields a reader never sees (``abstract``, ``keywords``,
  ``file``, ``date-added``, ``date-modified``, ``issn``) dropped, and any
  empty field;
* a page range written with a double hyphen (``1 - 68``, ``225–287`` and
  ``S1-S20`` become ``1--68``, ``225--287`` and ``S1--S20``); a value that is
  not plainly one range per comma-separated part is left as it is;
* a DOI written as a URL or with a ``doi:`` prefix reduced to the bare DOI.

Values are otherwise kept byte for byte: no LaTeX is decoded or encoded, no
title re-cased, no name reformatted. A key held by one series is taken as it
is. A key held by several series whose normalised copies are identical is
taken once. A key whose copies differ is a CONFLICT, and needs a ruling in
``sync/bib.yml``:

    rulings:
      Clarke_71:
        take: intermediate          # the copy the result starts from
        set: {pages: 17--33}        # fields to set (optional)
        drop: [volume]              # fields to drop (optional)
        type: book                  # entry type to set (optional)
        because: the publisher's record; owner's ruling of 2026-11-02 (#10)
        seal:                       # each series' copy when the ruling was made
          intermediate: 0c1d2e3f4a5b6c7d
          intro: 8e9f0a1b2c3d4e5f

The SEAL is the digest of each series' normalised copy at ruling time. A
ruling holds while the copy it takes is exactly its sealed version and every
other copy is its sealed version or already equal to the result. Any other
edit, or a series that starts holding the key with a different copy, REOPENS
the ruling, so a change upstream is never folded in silently. A ruling may
also fix a key held by one series (a style fix the normalising cannot make).
``--propose`` prints a stub, sealed against the current copies, for every
refused key, keeping a reopened ruling's take, set, drop and type.

What it refuses
---------------
Anything it cannot read exactly, rather than guess: a file that is not UTF-8,
a syntax error, a duplicate key, a field repeated in one entry (ignoring
case), a field name that is not one, ``@string`` or ``@preamble``, a bare
macro other than a month, a ``#`` concatenation, an unbalanced quoted value,
or a file in which bibtexparser and pybtex (the reader Jupyter Book 1 uses)
find different keys. A refused FILE stops the run (exit 2) and nothing is
written. A refused KEY (a conflict without a ruling, or a reopened ruling)
does not hold the others back: it keeps the entry the committed file already
has, if any, and the run exits 2 with the key and the reason. Nothing is ever
written that does not read back as the same entries, in both readers.

The committed file
------------------
Its header records a digest of each series' normalised entries, of
``sync/bib.yml``, and of the file's own entries. The check uses them to tell
the outcomes apart: a file whose entries no longer match their recorded digest
was edited by hand, and a recorded ``sync/bib.yml`` digest that is not the
current one is a ruling change that was not regenerated (both violations,
whatever else moved); a series whose entries moved is a refresh. A byte change
upstream that the normalising removes moves nothing.

Checks
------
* citation closure: every key a pool lecture cites resolves in the merged
  file (an error); keys no lecture of any series cites are listed (info);
* style: the non-mechanical rules the normalising cannot apply — the fields
  each entry type needs, names joined by ``and`` (no ``&`` or ``;``, no stray
  or extra commas), a DOI that starts ``10.``, keys unique when case is
  ignored (errors); LaTeX escapes (rule 11), unknown fields, and names that
  look like two people separated by a comma (info);
* equivalence: pybtex reads every merged entry as it reads the series copy the
  entry came from, apart from the normalising and the entry's ruling (an error
  otherwise). bibtexparser only splits the files; pybtex is an independent
  reader of both sides.

Usage
-----
    tools/bib-merge                  # check: regenerate in memory, compare with
                                     # the committed file, run the checks
    tools/bib-merge --write          # write lectures/_static/quant-econ.bib
    tools/bib-merge --report [PATH]  # the conflicts, side by side (markdown)
    tools/bib-merge --propose        # sync/bib.yml stubs for every refused key
    tools/bib-merge --reconstruct    # rebuild mirror/ for every series first

With ``--report`` to stdout or ``--propose``, the check's own lines go to
stderr. Exit status: 0 clean (info only); 1 the committed file is behind the
series' entries (run --write); 2 anything else: a refused file or key, a
failed check, a violation, a missing file, or an internal error. Under GitHub
Actions the outcome is also written to ``$GITHUB_OUTPUT``: ``bib_state``
(clean, refresh, violation, missing or error), ``bib_failed`` and
``bib_refresh`` (true or false).
"""

